Subject: [PATCH 2/8] =?UTF-8?q?feat(encode/nvenc):=20name=20who=20did=20th?=
 =?UTF-8?q?e=20RGB=E2=86=92YUV=20conversion,=20and=20let=20the=20VUI=20be?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20pinned?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The colour signalling is written from `hdr` alone, on the premise stated right
above it — "the input is already CSC'd to a specific matrix, so the stream must
say so". That premise holds for the NV12 and P010 paths, where a shader of ours
converts against a matrix we chose and the VUI merely reports it.

It does not hold for a packed-RGB input. There NVENC performs the conversion
internally, against a matrix and a swing the SDK neither documents nor derives
from these fields, and the stream still declares BT.2020 NCL limited because
`hdr` is set. Nothing fails loudly: the frame paces, encodes and decodes, and
comes out uniformly tinted because the decoder inverts with the label rather
than with the matrix that was actually used. On Linux that is every HDR
gamescope session (ARGB10 in); on Windows it is the ABGR10 path, while P010
stays correct.

This does not guess which value is right — guessing is how the current one got
there. It adds:

- `nvenc_internal_csc` on LowLatencyConfig, set from the buffer format at both
  call sites, so the distinction is stated where the decision is made rather
  than inferred from `hdr`;
- one INFO line naming the primaries/transfer/matrix/swing actually written,
  next to who converted — enough to read against a picture on the glass;
- `PUNKTFUNK_VUI_MATRIX` / `PUNKTFUNK_VUI_FULL_RANGE`, so the observed behaviour
  can be pinned on a real decoder instead of assumed from the header.

Both overrides default to the derived values, so an unset host is byte-for-byte
unchanged — asserted by a test, alongside the name→CICP mapping.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
